WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.000
A nematode and an earthworm look a lot alike.

2
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.740
Both are long, soft worms, with a gut that runs from mouth to anus.

3
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.640
Yet gene comparisons put them on different branches of the animal tree.

4
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.760
The nematode groups with insects, and other animals that moult.

5
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.840
The earthworm groups with snails and flatworms.

6
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.640
Body-plan characters are how we describe an animal.

7
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:30.340
On their own, they do not tell us who is related to whom.

8
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.180
Libbie Hyman's great survey of the invertebrates sorted these animals by body

9
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.540
cavity. Acoelomates, like a planarian flatworm, have tissue between gut and body

10
00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:46.840
wall. Pseudocoelomates, like a nematode, have a cavity without a full mesoderm

11
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:52.180
lining. Coelomates, like the earthworm, have a cavity lined entirely by mesoderm.

12
00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.200
A group like this is a grade, a shared level of organization.

13
00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.200
A clade is different.

14
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.320
It is an ancestor and all of its descendants.

15
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:10.010
In 1997, Aguinaldo, Lake and colleagues compared the 18S rRNA gene across animals.

16
00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:15.510
Nematodes grouped with arthropods and other moulting animals, not with annelids.

17
00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.670
Both moult a cuticle, so the group is called Ecdysozoa.

18
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:23.330
Later analyses of many genes broadly support it.

19
00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:29.230
They put flatworms inside Spiralia, with annelids and molluscs, among relatives that

20
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:33.430
have a coelom. So body cavity describes a grade, not a clade.

21
00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.750
These characters are still the right way to describe an animal.

22
00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.990
Symmetry: none, as in a sponge, or radial, or bilateral.

23
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.710
Germ layers: two, or three, with a middle layer called mesoderm.

24
00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.850
A body cavity, or none.

25
00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:53.330
And keep the gut apart from the cavity: even a planarian has a gut.

26
00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:56.763
These characters shape how an animal lives.

27
00:01:57.663 --> 00:02:02.523
A through gut moves food one way, so each region can do its own job, from a grinding

28
00:02:02.523 --> 00:02:03.963
gizzard to a long intestine.

29
00:02:04.983 --> 00:02:08.603
A sealed, fluid-filled segment works as a hydrostatic skeleton.

30
00:02:09.623 --> 00:02:12.663
Squeeze it narrower, and at constant volume it gets longer.

31
00:02:13.803 --> 00:02:15.543
Waves of this push an earthworm through the soil.

32
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:19.213
Old developmental rules need the same test.

33
00:02:20.053 --> 00:02:24.713
In a slipper snail, traced cells showed that the blastopore becomes the mouth, as

34
00:02:24.713 --> 00:02:26.373
the old mouth-first rule predicts.

35
00:02:27.253 --> 00:02:32.653
But blastopore fate varies, even among close relatives, so it is not a reliable test

36
00:02:32.653 --> 00:02:33.653
of the major clades.

37
00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:39.493
To test a grouping, sample many genes, and add independent evidence, such as which

38
00:02:39.493 --> 00:02:40.753
genes share chromosomes.

39
00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:43.813
Body-plan characters describe animals.

40
00:02:43.813 --> 00:02:46.913
Independent evidence tests their ancestry.
